<!--
    The menu tab's glyph: four rules, evenly spaced, on a 24 unit grid.

    ### Why this is drawn here rather than converted from the archive

    `design/ui-icons` carries the Phosphor set and the traced TradingView set, and neither has a
    plain list rule at the weight the bottom bar uses. This is four rounded rectangles; converting
    somebody else's would import a licence, a provenance note and a build step for a shape that is
    fully described by its own numbers.

    ### The numbers

    Four bars rather than three. Three reads as a hamburger, which on Android is a *drawer* — a
    surface that slides in from the edge — and this tab opens a page. Four reads as a list, which
    is what the menu is: a directory of named rows.

    Four rules 2 units tall with a 1 unit radius, 18 units wide, inset 3 units from each side. The
    pitch is 5, so their centres are at 4.5, 9.5, 14.5 and 19.5 — symmetric about the glyph's own
    centre at 12, with the ink spanning 3.5 to 20.5. A 2-unit rule on a 5-unit pitch is 40% ink,
    which is where the rest of this set sits: denser reads as a barcode at 24 dp, sparser does not
    read as a list at all.

    ### One weight, and that is deliberate

    Every other tab in this bar has an outline and a filled variant, because every other tab shows
    something that is happening. Nothing on the menu is live. A filled variant here would make the
    quietest destination in the bar the loudest shape in it whenever it is selected, and the bar
    marks its selection with type weight and a plate anyway. See `AppChrome.icon`.
-->
